# Strategic Quitting

Strategic quitting is the deliberate practice of recognizing when to walk away from a commitment or endeavor as a means of optimizing long-term outcomes. Unlike impulsive or reactive quitting, it is a skill that can be developed through reflection on timing, framing, and the acceptance of imperfection.

This concept builds on [[the-art-of-quitting]] and [[embracing-imperfection]]. It directly connects to [[decision-quality-vs-outcome-quality]] — a well-timed quit can be a high-quality decision even if it feels like failure in the moment. It also relates to [[framing-choices-for-success]], where how we frame a decision determines whether quitting feels like loss or liberation.

Duke's framework positions strategic quitting as complementary to [[systematic-decision-making]] and [[decision-tracking]], offering a structured approach to one of the most emotionally difficult decisions people face.
